//Move Constructor

A move constructor creates a new object by taking/stealing resources from a temporary (or otherwise movable) object instead of copying them.

This is useful when a class owns dynamically allocated memory or other resources.

Example:

class Student {
public:
    int* age;

    Student(int a) {
        age = new int(a);
    }

    Student(Student&& s) {
        age = s.age;
        s.age = nullptr;
    }
};

Suppose:

Student s1(20);
Student s2 = std::move(s1);

Instead of allocating new memory and copying 20, s2 takes the memory owned by s1.

Before:

s1 → [20]
s2

After:

s1 → nullptr

s2 → [20]
Copy vs Move

Copy constructor:

s1 → [20]
s2 → [20]

Creates a separate copy.

Move constructor:

s1 → nullptr
s2 ─────→ [20]

Transfers the resource.

Why use it?

It can avoid expensive copying and improve performance when transferring resources.

Simple definition:

Move constructor = constructor that transfers resources from one object to a new object instead of copying them.